Edit `ShipmentTracker.Client/Layout/NavMenu.razor` to replace Counter and Weather nav links with:
- Track Shipment (/track) with a search icon
- Shipments Dashboard (/shipments) with a list icon

Look at the existing NavMenu.razor to see its structure, then replace the Counter and Weather NavLink items.

## 11. Create EF Core migration

After all file changes, run:
```
cd /tmp/sv-22a416f3b7c447a6903e3ca405a85260/ShipmentTracker/ShipmentTracker
dotnet ef migrations add AddShipments --output-dir Data/Migrations
```
If `dotnet ef` is not available, install it first:
```
dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef
```

## 12. Add database auto-migration to Program.cs

In the server Program.cs, after `var app = builder.Build();` add:
```csharp
using (var scope = app.Services.CreateScope())
{
    var db = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ApplicationDbContext>();
    db.Database.Migrate();
}
```

## Important Notes:
- The server project references the Client project already (check the .csproj), so it can use Client types.
- Make sure all usings are correct.
- The `StatusUpdate` record should be defined at the end of Program.cs after `app.Run();` or in a separate file. A simpler approach: define it inline in the endpoint lambda or create a small file `ShipmentTracker/Models/StatusUpdate.cs`.
- The track endpoint is AllowAnonymous so customers don't need to log in to track.

Do all file operations (delete, create, edit) to make this work. Then run `dotnet build` on the solution to verify it compiles.

